## Pitfalls

- **Measuring what's easy instead of what matters.** Token cost and load time are easy to measure; factual error rate and source verification are what matter. Don't substitute one for the other.
- **Circular validation.** Measuring a metric using a script you wrote against data you generated produces 100% scores that mean nothing. Always test against real, independent data.
- **Marking "contract exists" as "problem solved."** A quality charter or scoring rubric is a structural artifact, not proof of quality. The gap between "written down" and "measured" is where most systems fail.
- **One-size-fits-all model selection.** Using the same model for every task wastes tokens on simple tasks and produces poor quality on complex ones. Route dynamically by task complexity.
- **Linear pipeline fragility.** Linear architectures (MetaGPT-style) collapse under blind trust faults (0% robustness). Iterative closed-loop designs (Table-Critic-style) neutralize 40%+ of faults that cause catastrophic collapse.
- **Ignoring semantic failures.** 75% of multi-agent failures are "silent gray errors" — no exception thrown, output looks valid, but content is factually wrong. Schema validation alone won't catch these.
- **Naive retry.** Retrying without preserving execution history wastes work. Semantic checkpointing enables informed re-computation from the last valid state.
- **Static model weights.** Routing weights configured once decay as models update. Continuously measure routing success rates and adjust weights based on accumulated data.

